\section{Specify What Clients Can Prove About Types}

A priority queue provides an \texttt{empty} value, an operation
that inserts a key, and a removal operation. If clients know the
queue is an array of keys, they can treat any array as a queue,
including arrays that violate the heap invariant. An abstract
type declaration lets the implementation use the array internally
without making that equation available to importers. The meaning
of an abstract type is not ``no representation exists'', but
``the client cannot derive a representation equality from this
signature''. The module treatment of abstraction and functors
in \cite{harper2016practical} gives a language-level account.

Signature matching checks more than the presence of member
names. Each abstract type specification is matched by a
declaration of the right kind and arity. Later member types use
those chosen declarations consistently. Manifest declarations
such as \texttt{type key = Bytes.t} impose an equality rather
than merely requiring a fresh name. An implementation with
\texttt{insert : Int.t -> queue -> queue} cannot match that
signature merely because both keys fit one machine word. Type
equality is not an ABI equivalence.

\section{Pass Ordering as a Module Parameter}

The example in the local OCaml text parameterizes ordered lists
by a module providing an element type and a comparison. We use
the same idea for priority queues. A functor takes an ordering
module and returns a queue module. The result publishes
\texttt{key = Order.key}, so a client can call \texttt{insert}
with its existing keys, but leaves \texttt{queue} abstract. The
comparator's ordering laws are a separate contract: a comparator
returning inconsistent results can break queue invariants without
producing any type error.
