Do not substitute the textual spelling \texttt{Order.key}
throughout the compiler. Elaborate it into a projection from
the formal module binder. Application substitutes a resolved
module path or identity for that binder; projections discovered
later must use the same substitution. Shadowing the spelling
\texttt{Order} in a nested module must not alter the type of
an already elaborated declaration.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Seal the Result With an Explicit Identity Rule}

\begin{verbatim}
signature ORDER:
    type key
    val compare : (key, key) -> Ordering

signature QUEUE(key_type):
    type key = key_type
    type queue
    val empty : queue
    val insert : (key, queue) -> queue
    val pop : queue -> Option<(key, queue)>

elaborate_apply(functor, arg, application_binder, mode):
    match_signature(arg, functor.parameter_signature)
    spec := substitute_module(functor.result_spec, arg.identity)
    if mode == Generative:
        result_id := fresh_static_stamp(application_binder)
    else:
        require_stable_path(functor, arg)
        result_id := intern_path_application(functor.id, arg.id)
    return seal_public_view(spec, result_id)

seal_public_view(spec, result_id):
    for component in spec.in_dependency_order:
        if component is abstract_type:
            publish_type_id(result_id, component.id)
        else:
            publish(component.with_public_type_projections())
\end{verbatim}

The functor definition is checked once against the parameter and
result signatures before these applications are elaborated.
Interning an applicative path creates a stable type identity, not
a cache of the functor's executed initialization or its values.
A generative rule makes fresh static identities at distinct
application binders in this toy elaborator. Repeated references
to one result binder reuse its identity. Do not mint a new
identity each time a type checker resolves a name.

\section{Decide Which Queues May Cross an Application Boundary}

Let \texttt{Forward} and \texttt{Reverse} share the same
integer key type but implement opposite orderings. A queue
from \texttt{MakeQueue(Forward)} must not be popped using the
reverse module: the first implementation arranged its storage
according to a different comparator. An applicative design keys
the abstract result type by the argument module identity, not
by the type of its keys or the shape of its signature. Conversely,
two stable aliases of \texttt{Forward} should not split result
types if the rule says aliases preserve identity.

\begin{verbatim}
A = MakeQueue(Forward)
B = MakeQueue(Forward)
C = MakeQueue(Reverse)

k  = 3                      // key shared across A, B, C
qa = A.insert(k, A.empty)    // accepted
C.pop(qa)                   // rejected under either rule
B.pop(qa)                   // applicative yes, generative no
\end{verbatim}

A language with first-class modules may represent hidden types
as existential packages. Unpacking exposes an abstract witness
locally; returning that witness as a naked type can be illegal
when its binder would escape. The check is about identifiers
occurring in types, not about whether memory is reachable at
run time. A private type projected from a named, exported
module can ordinarily escape by its public abstract name.

Keep a regression fixture that swaps the queue representation
while retaining its signature. Client code should still typecheck,
including code that passes keys from a separate package. Then
deliberately expose a representation equality and check that
tests using it distinguish the old and new versions. That
second fixture demonstrates the cost of publishing an equation:
discarding it later can break clients even though every value
function still has the same spelling and arity.

\section{Publish a Sharing Constraint Rather Than a Representation}

Suppose two modules are passed to a new functor, one that
prints keys and one that provides the ordering. Testing the
signatures independently establishes that both define a key
type. It does not establish that the key removed from the
queue can be printed. The new functor must either demand a
sharing equation or accept a conversion as a value argument.
The equation can connect two abstract types without making
either one equal to any client-visible representation.

\begin{verbatim}
functor Describe(Order : ORDER,
                 Print : PRINTER where Print.key = Order.key):
    module Q = MakeQueue(Order)
    val describe_top(q : Q.queue):
        match Q.pop(q):
            None -> "empty"
            Some(k, rest) -> Print.format(k)
\end{verbatim}

At application the checker resolves both actual projections,
then tests them in the public type-equality environment. If
the printer and ordering each hide their own integer type,
the checker cannot use their private equations to conclude
that they agree. To expose the right fact, define the abstract
key in a shared module and refine both signatures to refer to
that same abstract key. The resulting diagnostic can point to
two distinct abstract declarations instead of naming two
unavailable representations.

Then change just the comparator without changing the shared
key type. Printing still works, but each queue remains tied to
its own ordered representation. These two fixtures exercise
different equalities: one for values contained in a queue, the
other for the abstract queue itself.
